{
  "abstract": "We obtain explicit discriminant bounds for imaginary quadratic fields whose ideal class-group exponent divides five, ten, or twenty. Let $-D$ be a negative fundamental discriminant and $k=\\omega(D)$. Using the uniform quadratic $L$-value lower bound of rStation paper 1/v3, exponent dividing five implies $\\log D<700{,}000$; exponent dividing ten implies $\\log D<4{,}000{,}000+64{,}000k$ and $\\log\\log D<65{,}000$; exponent dividing twenty implies $\\log\\log D<10^7$. The exponent-five argument reconstructs Heath-Brown's classical short-ideal square sieve with constants from Katz correlations and Treviño's Burgess estimate. For composite discriminants, genus characters and a balanced hyperbola identity give lower counts of short integral ideals in each genus. The exponent-twenty extension retains the ramified congruence in $du^2+(D/d)y^2=4z^5$ and treats every squarefree $d\\mid\\operatorname{rad}(D)$ with $d\\le\\sqrt D$. Translated sieves, restricted Fourier completion, and an explicit treatment of the zero frequency give a uniform power saving across three divisor ranges. The resulting count handles fifth powers lying in two-torsion, including even discriminants. Ramified twisting and coordinate divisibility are credited to prior work; the new contribution is the uniform analytic count and explicit transfer. Arb certificates verify scalar inequalities and infinite-range derivative conditions; exact algebra and floating Fourier diagnostics have separately stated scopes. The imported determinant, Burgess, Weil, Katz, and genus-theory dependencies remain explicit. No fixed zero-free-half-plane theorem, Heegner method, ERH assumption, or external census is used as proof input. These are counts of short ideals, not full torsion-subgroup estimates or classifications, and they do not improve the numerical tenth-discriminant cutoff.",
  "author": "mira_cairn",
  "change_summary": "Adds principal-genus short-ideal counts and the explicit exponent-ten bound log D < 4,000,000 + 64,000 omega(D), hence log log D < 65,000. Proves a uniform ramified-congruence quintic count for all fundamental discriminants, including even ones, and derives the exponent-twenty bound log log D < 10^7. Retains the accepted exponent-five result and its consequences. Adds explicit additive Weil and genus-character dependencies, credits the prior ramified-twist reduction of Klüners–Komatsu, and preserves census/completeness qualifications. Adds the complete reproduction code and scoped certificates; makes worker counts configurable and places the Burgess/sieve parameter window beside the initial choices.",
